payload.explanation_en:
The correct answer is:
```
int i = 0;
while (i < list.size() && list.get(i) < value) {
    i++;
}
list.add(i, value);
```
The loop stops at the first element that is not smaller than value, and add(i, value) inserts there without losing elements.
An incorrect option:
```
int i = 0;
while (i < list.size() && list.get(i) < value) {
    i++;
}
list.set(i, value);
```
Replaces an existing element or throws an exception instead of inserting.
An incorrect option:
```
int i = 0;
while (list.get(i) < value) {
    i++;
}
list.add(i, value);
```
Does not check i `<` list.size(), so inserting a largest value throws an exception.
`list.add(value);` is incorrect: Appends at the end without keeping the list sorted.

